&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is part 4 of 8 of the free series &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/series/frontend-system-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Frontend system design for enterprise applications&lt;/a&gt;, first published on &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-rendering-csr-ssr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tt-tech.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A team adds server-side rendering to an internal banking dashboard. Every page needs a logged-in user, no search engine will ever see it, and now there is a Node server to run, patch and audit. The first load is a little faster. The bill and the attack surface are a lot bigger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Many logged-in enterprise apps do not need server rendering. Some parts of the same organisation do. The decision is per page, not per company.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  CSR for the app, SSR or prerender for the public pages
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Angular's own guide is honest about both sides. Client-side rendering "has the simplest development model", and "the server does not need to do any work to render a page beyond serving static JavaScript assets". It "generally has worse performance", and "may negatively affect" SEO for pages that crawlers index. Server-side rendering "may increase server hosting costs" and "requires you to author code that does not strictly depend on browser APIs".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/ssr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[1]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fv70ukyjil8ijkb6cit2e.webp" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fv70ukyjil8ijkb6cit2e.webp" alt="Four rendering options on a line from HTML made on the server to HTML made in the browser: server rendering, prerender, SSR with hydration (with incremental hydration as a variant) and client rendering, each with where the HTML is made, one plus and one minus. Below, public pages are prerendered or server-rendered, and pages behind the login use client rendering" width="799" height="485"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Image: tt-tech.io, adapted from web.dev, &lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/rendering-on-the-web" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Rendering on the web&lt;/a&gt;, CC BY 4.0.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the line I draw is the login. Behind it (dashboards, back office, case handling), CSR. In front of it (product pages, rates, branch finder), prerender what is the same for everyone and server-render what changes per request. web.dev says the same in general terms: "you can always choose to use server-side rendering for some pages and not others."&lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/rendering-on-the-web" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[2]&lt;/a&gt; Angular supports that per route with &lt;code&gt;RenderMode.Client&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Prerender&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;Server&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/ssr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[1]&lt;/a&gt; Often the public site and the app are simply two apps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;CSR or SSR, per page&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Behind a login? Yes: &lt;strong&gt;CSR&lt;/strong&gt;, static assets only.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Not behind a login, and the same HTML for everyone? &lt;strong&gt;Prerender&lt;/strong&gt;, HTML at build time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Not behind a login, and HTML that differs per request? &lt;strong&gt;SSR&lt;/strong&gt;, HTML per request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The decision tree as a diagram is in the &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-rendering-csr-ssr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;article on tt-tech.io&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Hydration is the price of SSR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server HTML still has to become an app. Angular's hydration reuses the server-rendered DOM. Without it, Angular destroys and re-renders that DOM, which "may result in a visible UI flicker".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/hydration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[3]&lt;/a&gt; But "event listeners are not attached until hydration completes", which is why Angular ships event replay.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Incremental hydration goes further: &lt;code&gt;@defer&lt;/code&gt; blocks with &lt;code&gt;hydrate&lt;/code&gt; triggers (idle, viewport, interaction) control when Angular loads and hydrates that part, which "can produce smaller initial bundles". It "is enabled by default when you use provideClientHydration()".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/incremental-hydration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[4]&lt;/a&gt; Worth it on a public page with a heavy widget. Not a reason to add SSR to a dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  &lt;code&gt;@defer&lt;/code&gt; for the heavy widgets
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Charts, rich-text editors and PDF viewers rarely need to be in the first bundle. &lt;code&gt;@defer&lt;/code&gt; blocks "reduce the initial bundle size of your application by deferring the loading of code that is not strictly necessary for the initial rendering of a page".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/templates/defer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[5]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One enterprise catch: deferred dependencies "must be standalone". Code that still lives in an &lt;code&gt;NgModule&lt;/code&gt; cannot be deferred. Your legacy corner decides what you can lazy-load.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In an old codebase, the legacy corner decides what you can lazy-load.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Lazy routes along team lines
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lazy routes split the bundle where your teams already split the code: one feature library, one route, one chunk. Angular notes that lazy routes remove "large portions of JavaScript from the initial bundle", recommends eager loading "for primary landing page(s)", and warns that deeply nested lazy loading "can significantly impact performance".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/best-practices/performance/lazy-loaded-routes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[6]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Zoneless and OnPush by default
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two defaults changed what triggers rendering in Angular. Zoneless "is the default in Angular v21+".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[7]&lt;/a&gt; OnPush "is the default change detection strategy in Angular (since v22)".&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/best-practices/skipping-subtrees" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[8]&lt;/a&gt; Rendering now follows notifications: a signal read in a template changes, an input is set, a template listener runs, or code calls &lt;code&gt;markForCheck()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[7]&lt;/a&gt; Legacy code that mutated a field and waited for Zone.js to notice now needs one of those.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  React Server Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the React side of the house, Server Components render "ahead of time, before bundling, in an environment separate from your client app or SSR server".&lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[9]&lt;/a&gt; They suit content-heavy pages that read data on the server. They usually come with Server Functions, which move a security line into the frontend repo: their arguments "are fully client-controlled", and React tells you to "validate that the logged-in user is allowed to perform that action".&lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-server" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[10]&lt;/a&gt; In a regulated app, that function is a public endpoint and gets reviewed like one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  On Atlas Bank
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Atlas Bank is client-rendered on purpose: logged-in banking, no SEO, no Node server. It is zoneless (the repo's &lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt; says "Never add &lt;code&gt;zone.js&lt;/code&gt;") and every feature is a lazy route in &lt;code&gt;apps/atlas/src/app/app.routes.ts&lt;/code&gt;, the legacy screens included. Two of the five routes:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'accounts'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadChildren&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@atlas/feature-accounts'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;accountsRoutes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'transfers'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadChildren&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@atlas/legacy-transfers'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;then&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;LegacyTransfersModule&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The legacy &lt;code&gt;NgModule&lt;/code&gt; can still be a lazy route; it just cannot sit inside a &lt;code&gt;@defer&lt;/code&gt; block. Even the dashboard on &lt;code&gt;''&lt;/code&gt; is lazy, against Angular's advice for landing pages. In a small demo the difference is small; in a real app I would measure it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I left out, and why
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Islands, resumability, streaming SSR as separate items.&lt;/strong&gt; Framework-specific variations on "send less JavaScript".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ISR.&lt;/strong&gt; A content-site technique, best known from Next.js.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Virtual DOM versus fine-grained reactivity.&lt;/strong&gt; A framework debate that rarely changes a product decision.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  On Monday
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;List your routes and mark each one: behind the login or not. Only the second group is an SSR question.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find your heaviest widget below the fold. Wrap it in &lt;code&gt;@defer (on viewport)&lt;/code&gt; and compare the initial bundle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Search for components that still rely on Zone.js to notice a change. They are your zoneless backlog.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/blog/frontend-performance-enterprise-budgets" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Part 5, on performance&lt;/a&gt;, is next.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  For your coding agent
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rules of this article, written for an agent. Paste them into your &lt;code&gt;AGENTS.md&lt;/code&gt;, save them as a skill, or point your agent at the file.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight markdown"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gu"&gt;# Rendering rules for enterprise apps
&lt;/span&gt;
Source: https://tt-tech.io/blog/enterprise-frontend-rendering-csr-ssr
For Angular and React frontends. Add to AGENTS.md or save as a skill. Your project's own rules win where they differ.

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Rules
&lt;/span&gt;
1. Decide rendering per page, not per app. The line is the login: pages behind it use client-side rendering (CSR) by default.
2. Do not add server-side rendering (SSR) to dashboards, back office or other pages behind a login by default. It adds a server to run, patch and audit.
3. For public pages, prerender what is the same for everyone and server-render what changes per request. In Angular, set it per route with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`RenderMode.Client`&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`RenderMode.Prerender`&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`RenderMode.Server`&lt;/span&gt;.
4. Code that runs under SSR must not depend on browser APIs. Keep hydration on with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`provideClientHydration()`&lt;/span&gt;, so Angular reuses the server DOM instead of re-rendering it.
5. Use incremental hydration (&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`@defer`&lt;/span&gt; with &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`hydrate`&lt;/span&gt; triggers such as idle, viewport or interaction) for a heavy widget on a public page. It is not a reason to add SSR.
6. Put heavy widgets below the fold (charts, rich-text editors, PDF viewers) in &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`@defer`&lt;/span&gt;, for example &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`@defer (on viewport)`&lt;/span&gt;.
7. Deferred dependencies must be standalone. Code that still lives in an &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`NgModule`&lt;/span&gt; cannot go in &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`@defer`&lt;/span&gt;; load it through a lazy route instead.
8. Split lazy routes along team lines: one feature library, one route, one chunk. Load primary landing pages eagerly, as Angular recommends, and avoid deeply nested lazy loading.
9. Never rely on Zone.js to notice a change. Trigger rendering through a signal read in the template, an input, a template listener, or &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`markForCheck()`&lt;/span&gt;.
10. Treat every React Server Function (&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`'use server'`&lt;/span&gt;) as a public endpoint. Its arguments are client-controlled: validate them and check that the logged-in user may perform the action.

&lt;span class="gu"&gt;## Before you say done
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;For every new or changed route, state whether it is behind the login and which render mode it uses.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;When you add or move a &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`@defer`&lt;/span&gt; block or a lazy route, run the production build and report the initial bundle size before and after.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;Search changed components for plain fields the template reads and code mutates. Make them signals, or name the notification that renders them.
&lt;span class="k"&gt;- &lt;/span&gt;Search the diff for &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`'use server'`&lt;/span&gt;. For each function, name where it validates its arguments and checks the user.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The same rules as a file: &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/agents/enterprise-frontend-rendering-csr-ssr.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://tt-tech.io/agents/enterprise-frontend-rendering-csr-ssr.md&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/ssr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Server-side and hybrid-rendering&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;web.dev, &lt;a href="https://web.dev/articles/rendering-on-the-web" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Rendering on the web&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/hydration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hydration&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/incremental-hydration" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Incremental Hydration&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/templates/defer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Deferred loading with @defer&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/best-practices/performance/lazy-loaded-routes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Lazy-loaded routes&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/guide/zoneless" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Angular without ZoneJS (Zoneless)&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Angular, &lt;a href="https://angular.dev/best-practices/skipping-subtrees" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Skipping component subtrees&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React, &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/server-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Server Components&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React, &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/rsc/use-server" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;'use server' directive&lt;/a&gt;, docs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part 4 of 8 of the free series &lt;a href="https://tt-tech.io/series/frontend-system-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Frontend system design for enterprise applications&lt;/a&gt; on tt-tech.io, also in French and Arabic.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm Gopi Gugan, a software engineer building AI features with FastAPI, Postgres/pgvector, and React/TypeScript. You can find more of my work at &lt;a href="https://www.gopigugan.ca/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.gopigugan.ca/&lt;/a&gt;. I recently shipped an LLM feature that worked well in testing — and then watched it quietly drift in production. Nothing crashed. The model just got a little worse, a little slower, a little more expensive, and nobody noticed for a week. That is the failure mode of LLM features: they do not fail loudly, they fade. So I built monitoring for it, and this is what I actually watch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Latency, cost, and errors — the boring three
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before anything clever, I track the same three things I would track for any API endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Latency:&lt;/strong&gt; p50 and p95 per request, split by model and by feature, so one slow prompt does not hide inside an average&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cost:&lt;/strong&gt; tokens in, tokens out, and dollars per request — logged with every call, not estimated later from a bill&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Errors:&lt;/strong&gt; provider timeouts, rate limits, and malformed responses, counted separately so I know which one is actually hurting me&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are boring, and they catch most of the real problems. A prompt that suddenly doubles in cost usually means the input is growing — someone pasted a whole document where a paragraph used to go. A latency spike is often a model version change, not my code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Log the prompts and the answers, not just the metrics
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Metrics tell me something is wrong. Traces tell me what. I log every LLM call with the prompt, the response, the model, the token counts, and how long it took — plus a request ID that ties it back to the user action that triggered it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep the full trace, not just the final answer — I need to see the exact prompt the model saw&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sample aggressively for high-volume features (I keep 10% of routine calls, 100% of failures and flagged ones), so logging never becomes the bottleneck&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Redact secrets and personal data before the trace leaves the request — once it is in a log store, it is hard to take back&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Store traces where I can search them, not in a file I will never open&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a user reports a bad answer, the first thing I do is pull the trace. Half the time the prompt was not what I expected — a missing instruction, a stale piece of context, a tool result that arrived empty.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Watch quality, not just uptime
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An LLM can be up, fast, and cheap while giving bad answers. That is the gap normal monitoring misses. So I run a small, continuous quality check on top of production traffic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Golden prompts:&lt;/strong&gt; a fixed set of 20–30 prompts I run against production every hour, scored by simple checks (did it refuse when it should, did it cite the right source, did it follow the format)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;User signals:&lt;/strong&gt; thumbs-down, "try again" clicks, and follow-up corrections — cheap signals that are worth more than my own judgement&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Drift alerts:&lt;/strong&gt; if the pass rate on golden prompts drops for an hour, I get notified the same way I would for a 500 error&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I keep the scoring simple on purpose. A hand-graded eval set run hourly beats a fancy automated judge I never look at. The point is a number that moves when the model's behaviour moves, not a perfect measurement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A small logging wrapper, in plain Python
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is roughly how I wrap every LLM call, so the metrics and traces come for free instead of being bolted on later:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;logging&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;logging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"llm"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;call_llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;monotonic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;llm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;chat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;latency_ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;monotonic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;info&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"llm_call"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;extra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"request_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"model"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"prompt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;prompt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"response"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tokens_in"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;input_tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"tokens_out"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;usage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;output_tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"latency_ms"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;latency_ms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reply&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;except&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Exception&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;logger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"llm_error"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;extra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"request_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"model"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"latency_ms"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;monotonic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;start&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The wrapper does three things: it measures, it logs the trace with the request ID, and it separates errors from slow successes. Everything downstream — dashboards, alerts, cost reports — reads from these logs. One wrapper, one source of truth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would do first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your LLM feature is already live and unmonitored, do not build a platform. Start with this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wrap every LLM call in one function that logs the prompt, response, tokens, latency, and request ID&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put latency, cost, and error rate on a dashboard you already check — next to your other endpoints, not in a separate tool&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pick 20 golden prompts and run them hourly, with an alert when the pass rate drops&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review a random sample of traces every week — the bad answers teach you more than the metrics&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LLM features fail quietly, so monitoring them is not optional polish — it is how you find out something went wrong before your users tell you. Log the traces, watch the quality, and keep a human in the loop on the alerts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ship the monitoring with the feature, not after it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask a coding agent for a pricing section and you get the same pricing section everyone else gets: three cards, the middle one highlighted, a gradient somewhere. Agents write UI from memory, and their memory averages out to a template.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Good UI code is already out there, free to use. shadcn/ui, Radix, Base UI, Magic UI, Kokonut UI, Aceternity UI and the shadcn-style registries around them have components that someone designed, tested and shipped. The agent just doesn't know where to look, and searching 40 sites by hand is not something you want to do for every screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Clay UI&lt;/a&gt; for that gap. It is an MIT-licensed CLI plus an agent skill. Your agent asks it for a component, picks from real matches, downloads the source and then rewrites it to fit your project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What it looks like
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install it in a project:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx @behavio1/clayui init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37ks0lprb5ny0ubbbvea.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37ks0lprb5ny0ubbbvea.jpg" alt="How Clay UI works: you ask, your agent reads the need, Clay UI searches a local index, you pick a variant, Clay UI downloads the source, and your agent shapes it into your project" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This installs a skill for Claude Code (and for Codex, Cursor and other agents that read &lt;code&gt;.agents/skills&lt;/code&gt;) and builds a local index of the libraries. After that you talk to your agent as usual:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Find me a data table with filters and make it match our dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent runs a search. Here is real output from version 0.5.0, with no API keys set:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;$ clayui search "data table with filters" --limit 4
beautifului/filter-table  [gaps: preview-unverified]
    Filter Table — Status chips that reorganize live data.
    preview: https://www.beautifului.dev/#filter-table
reui/data-grid-column-filter  [complete]
    Data Grid Column Filter
    preview: https://reui.io/docs/data-grid-column-filter
blocks-so/table-03  [gaps: no-preview]
    Table with Filters — A table with filters block.
    preview: none
uiarc/filter-toolbar  [complete]
    Filter toolbar — Keep collection filters close and easy to reset.
    preview: https://uiarc.dev/components/filter-toolbar
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You open the preview links, pick one, and the agent runs &lt;code&gt;clayui get &amp;lt;id&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. The source lands in a local folder as an example. The agent then rewrites it for your stack, tokens and style. Clay UI never installs a component or a dependency into your project. What ends up in your codebase is code your agent wrote, starting from something that already worked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Before and after
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We rebuilt five screens of a made-up app this way. The left side is a typical template; the right side uses components Clay UI found: &lt;code&gt;aceternity/hero-highlight&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;avara/pricing-section&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;spectrum/insight-cards&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;devl/split-sign-up&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;magicui/bento-grid&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F988n758y67nym08r90rj.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F988n758y67nym08r90rj.jpg" alt="Five screens before and after" width="800" height="1251"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a 53-second film of the same comparison on the &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one/#before-after" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;website&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A few decisions we made
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Licenses first.&lt;/strong&gt; A library only goes into the index if its license allows passing the source on and its code can be fetched by a machine. React Bits, Animate UI, shadcnblocks and shadcn studio are out because their licenses forbid redistribution. Prism UI is out because it is AGPL. The README lists every library we reviewed and left out, with the reason. Downloaded components keep their authors' terms, so keep their notices when you adapt them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Local and keyless by default.&lt;/strong&gt; The index lives on your machine, and search works right after install using keywords. If you add a Gemini API key, it switches to semantic search; only then does your query go to Gemini to be embedded. No account is needed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A small native binary.&lt;/strong&gt; The CLI is written in Rust. &lt;code&gt;npx&lt;/code&gt; fetches the right binary for macOS, Linux or Windows, so you need Node 18.20.8+ and nothing else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The agent stays in charge.&lt;/strong&gt; Clay UI only finds and downloads. Matching your design system, tokens and naming stays the agent's job, with you reviewing the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx @behavio1/clayui init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Website: &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://clayui.behavio.one&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a library you like is missing, open an issue with a link to it and how its components can be fetched. We would also like to hear where search gives you bad matches. How we measure search quality is written up in &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui/blob/main/docs/search-tuning.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;docs/search-tuning.md&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: we are the team that builds Clay UI. This article was written with help from Claude. The search output above is from a real run of Clay UI 0.5.0.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When I started building Carrel, a real-time collaborative CodeMirror 6 workspace, I thought auth was the easy part: hash room passcodes with &lt;code&gt;scrypt&lt;/code&gt;, verify on join, done.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I tested what happens under brute-force traffic. &lt;code&gt;scrypt&lt;/code&gt; is slow on purpose. Run it synchronously and it blocks Node's event loop; run dozens of async &lt;code&gt;scrypt&lt;/code&gt; calls uncapped and they saturate libuv's threadpool. Either way, anyone hammering wrong passcodes starts stuttering live keystrokes for everyone typing in the room.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how I ended up structuring the server and protocol across the 3-package TypeScript monorepo (&lt;code&gt;@carrel/shared&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;@carrel/server&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;@carrel/client&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Bounded scrypt + single-use WS tickets
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;server/src/throttle.ts&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;POST /api/rooms/:id/ticket&lt;/code&gt; runs async &lt;code&gt;scrypt&lt;/code&gt; inside a 4-slot concurrency limiter paired with per-IP progressive lockouts (and ignores &lt;code&gt;X-Forwarded-For&lt;/code&gt; unless &lt;code&gt;TRUST_PROXY&lt;/code&gt; is explicitly enabled). Once verified, the client gets a short-lived, single-use nonce-bound HMAC ticket to upgrade &lt;code&gt;/ws&lt;/code&gt;, which gets burned immediately on upgrade so replay attempts fail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. One WebSocket with a 1-byte frame header
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of pushing everything through JSON messages or opening parallel sockets, &lt;code&gt;shared/src/frames.ts&lt;/code&gt; prefixes every binary WebSocket payload with one routing byte:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[0x00] -&amp;gt; raw y-protocols/sync CRDT updates (Y.Doc persisted via SQLite WAL)
[0x01] -&amp;gt; coalesced awareness updates (remote carets, selections, 80ms lerp)
[0x02] -&amp;gt; typed JSON control events (role changes, kicks, host handover)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;CodeMirror 6 renders remote carets with an 80ms interpolation curve (&lt;code&gt;CarrelProvider.ts&lt;/code&gt;) and turns interpolation off automatically when &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt; is set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Not losing room admin on a 2-second Wi-Fi drop
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a host refreshes a tab or drops Wi-Fi briefly, promoting someone else on the exact &lt;code&gt;close&lt;/code&gt; event creates constant role churn. In &lt;code&gt;server/src/host.ts&lt;/code&gt;, a &lt;code&gt;HOST_GRACE_MS&lt;/code&gt; (5000ms) timer marks the seat &lt;code&gt;hostPending&lt;/code&gt; first. Only if that timer expires does seniority election promote the next peer by &lt;code&gt;min(joinedAt)&lt;/code&gt; (tie-broken by &lt;code&gt;min(clientId)&lt;/code&gt;), while &lt;code&gt;SENIORITY_WINDOW_MS&lt;/code&gt; (30s) restores standing on quick reconnects. Multi-tab promotion and creator-key reclamation are verified in Playwright E2E specs (&lt;code&gt;phase5.test.ts&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full architecture notes and failure-mode diagrams: &lt;a href="https://theayush.pages.dev/projects/carrel" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://theayush.pages.dev/projects/carrel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Code on GitHub: &lt;a href="https://github.com/theayushagarwal/carrel" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/theayushagarwal/carrel&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js 16.4.4 shipped a backported fix that puts the CSP nonce on the Partial Prerendering (PPR) resume bootstrap, the small inline script that wakes up the dynamic part of a prerendered page. If you run a strict, nonce based Content Security Policy on top of PPR, that script has to carry the nonce your policy expects, or the browser is supposed to block it. Here's how I'd reproduce the problem on your current version, prove the patch fixes it on yours, and wire a check into CI so it never sneaks back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;16.4.4 is a patch release with two backports: a Turbopack startup CSS update fix and the CSP nonce fix for the PPR resume bootstrap. It is not the canary feature set, so don't upgrade expecting anything else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A nonce only protects you if every script the framework emits carries it. Verify that against the raw HTML and in a real browser, not by reading the release note.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put a two part check in CI: every executable script in the HTML has the response nonce, and a headless browser reports zero CSP violations on your PPR routes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What changed and what is actually available
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/vercel/next.js/releases/tag/v16.4.4" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js 16.4.4 release notes&lt;/a&gt; list two backports: one for Turbopack startup CSS updates and one described as a CSP nonce fix for the PPR resume bootstrap. That's the whole verified surface. The release explicitly does not bring in everything that's on canary, so if a feature you saw in a canary changelog isn't in that note, assume it isn't in 16.4.4.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick refresher on why one script matters. A nonce is a random value your server generates for every request. It goes into the &lt;code&gt;Content-Security-Policy&lt;/code&gt; header (&lt;code&gt;script-src 'nonce-&amp;lt;value&amp;gt;'&lt;/code&gt;) and onto each &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tag, and the browser only runs scripts whose nonce matches. &lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Security/Practical_implementation_guides/CSP" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN's practical CSP implementation guide&lt;/a&gt; recommends exactly this, nonce based strict CSP for dynamically generated content, hashes for static content, and &lt;code&gt;strict-dynamic&lt;/code&gt; where appropriate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PPR adds a twist. The static shell of a route is prerendered, and the dynamic holes are filled in at request time. Something has to kick off that resume step in the browser, and that something is an inline bootstrap script. The release note calls the fix a nonce fix for that bootstrap, so the practical question for you is simple: on your current version, does that script carry your nonce, and on 16.4.4, does it?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fng2o4bp5so46438cp2su.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fng2o4bp5so46438cp2su.gif" alt="A request flows through the proxy, which mints a nonce and sets the CSP header; the prerendered static shell is served, then the resume bootstrap script must carry the same nonce before the dynamic holes stream in" width="760" height="428"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You want a clean repro app for this, separate from your real one, so nothing else in your config muddies the result. If you're setting one up on a fresh machine, &lt;a href="https://mudassirkhan.me/blog/install-next-js" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;my walkthrough on installing Next.js on Windows, Mac and Linux&lt;/a&gt; covers the toolchain. After that, create the app and pin the exact version you run in production today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Implementation details and limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the nonce wiring I'd use for the repro. It mints a nonce per request, sends the policy on the response, and also passes it on the request headers, because that's where Next.js reads it from when it stamps its own scripts.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// proxy.ts (Next.js 16). On apps still using middleware.ts, export the same function as `middleware`.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next/server"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;proxy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nonce&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;btoa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isDev&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;NODE_ENV&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"development"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`default-src 'self'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`script-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}' 'strict-dynamic'${isDev ? " 'unsafe-eval'" : ""}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`style-src 'self' 'nonce-${nonce}'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`img-src 'self' blob: data:`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`object-src 'none'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`base-uri 'self'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`frame-ancestors 'none'`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"; "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestHeaders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestHeaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"x-nonce"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;nonce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestHeaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Security-Policy"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;NextResponse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requestHeaders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Security-Policy"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;matcher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|favicon.ico).*)"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;missing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"header"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next-router-prefetch"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"header"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"purpose"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"prefetch"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then a route that actually exercises PPR: a static heading in the shell and one component that reads cookies, so it can only render at request time.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/ppr-check/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cookies&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next/headers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Personalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cookies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;store&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"theme"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"default"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;testid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dynamic-hole"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Theme&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cookie&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;theme&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;shell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Loading&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;personalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Personalized&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three limits worth knowing before you trust any result:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Test the production build.&lt;/strong&gt; Run &lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;next start&lt;/code&gt;. The dev server needs &lt;code&gt;'unsafe-eval'&lt;/code&gt; and behaves differently enough that a clean dev run proves nothing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nonces need request time HTML.&lt;/strong&gt; A per request value can't live inside HTML that was generated once at build time. For pages that are fully static, MDN's advice is hashes, not nonces.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The spec is still moving.&lt;/strong&gt; W3C's latest CSP Level 3 document is a Working Draft, not a final Recommendation, so how browsers treat &lt;code&gt;strict-dynamic&lt;/code&gt; and friends follows what they've implemented. Test in the browsers you actually support.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Evidence and verification checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Don't take the release note as the test. Run the same checks on your current version and on 16.4.4 and compare.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsw4utslg2ovjku67imtu.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsw4utslg2ovjku67imtu.gif" alt="Before the backport, the resume bootstrap script has no matching nonce and the browser blocks it; on 16.4.4 the script carries the request nonce and runs, so the dynamic hole fills in" width="720" height="405"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check 1: the raw HTML.&lt;/strong&gt; This script fetches a route, pulls the nonce out of the response header and fails if any executable &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;script&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tag doesn't carry it. Because &lt;code&gt;res.text()&lt;/code&gt; waits for the whole streamed response, it also sees the scripts that arrive with the dynamic part.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// verify-nonce.mjs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// usage: node verify-nonce.mjs http://localhost:3000/ppr-check&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;argv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"http://localhost:3000/ppr-check"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;accept&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"text/html"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"content-security-policy"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;csp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'nonce-([^'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/);
if (!match) {
  console.error("FAIL: no nonce in the Content-Security-Policy header");
  process.exit(1);
}
const nonce = match[1];

const html = await res.text();
const dataTypes = /type="application\/(ld\+)?json"/i;
const scripts = [...html.matchAll(/&amp;lt;script\b[^&amp;gt;]*&amp;gt;/gi)]
  .map((m) =&amp;gt; m[0])
  .filter((tag) =&amp;gt; !dataTypes.test(tag));

const missing = scripts.filter((tag) =&amp;gt; {
  const found = tag.match(/\bnonce="([^"]*)"/);
  return !found || found[1] !== nonce;
});

console.log(JSON.stringify({ url, scripts: scripts.length, missing: missing.length }, null, 2));
if (missing.length &amp;gt; 0) {
  for (const tag of missing) console.error("MISSING NONCE:", tag.slice(0, 160));
  process.exit(1);
}
console.log("PASS: every executable script carries the response nonce");
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check 2: a real browser.&lt;/strong&gt; The HTML check tells you what was sent. The browser tells you what was blocked. This Playwright spec listens for &lt;code&gt;securitypolicyviolation&lt;/code&gt; events and also asserts that the dynamic hole actually rendered.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// tests/csp.spec.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@playwright/test"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"PPR route renders with zero CSP violations"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;on&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"console"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"error"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;startsWith&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"CSP_VIOLATION"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;msg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addInitScript&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;addEventListener&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"securitypolicyviolation"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`CSP_VIOLATION ${event.violatedDirective} ${event.blockedURI}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;goto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/ppr-check"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getByTestId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"dynamic-hole"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBeVisible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;waitForLoadState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"networkidle"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;violations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toEqual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Check 3: compare versions.&lt;/strong&gt; Same app, two installs:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# baseline: the 16.4.x you run today&lt;/span&gt;
npm install next@&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"$CURRENT_NEXT"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; npx next build &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;npx next start -p 3000 &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; sleep 5
node verify-nonce.mjs http://localhost:3000/ppr-check&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; npx playwright &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; tests/csp.spec.ts
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;kill&lt;/span&gt; %1 2&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;/dev/null &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; pkill -f &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"next start"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# patched&lt;/span&gt;
npm install next@16.4.4 &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; npx next build &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;npx next start -p 3000 &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; sleep 5
node verify-nonce.mjs http://localhost:3000/ppr-check&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; npx playwright &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; tests/csp.spec.ts
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the baseline already passes both checks, your setup wasn't hitting that path, which is good to know, and the upgrade is still a cheap patch bump. If the baseline fails and 16.4.4 passes, you've reproduced the bug and verified the backport in one go.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5rc05jrggnvr2x64obeb.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5rc05jrggnvr2x64obeb.gif" alt="Three verification checks build one after another: the header carries a nonce, every executable script carries the same nonce, and the browser reports zero violations while the dynamic hole renders" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where rollout or governance fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The patch is the easy part. These are the ways I'd expect a CSP rollout to go wrong anyway:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One app upgraded, the rest forgotten.&lt;/strong&gt; In a monorepo it's easy to bump the app you tested and leave three others on the old 16.4 patch. Check every lockfile, not just the one in front of you.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;'unsafe-inline'&lt;/code&gt; as a quick fix.&lt;/strong&gt; When a script gets blocked, adding it feels like progress. It quietly undoes the reason you have a nonce. Fix the script, don't widen the policy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A CDN caching nonce HTML.&lt;/strong&gt; If a shared cache stores the HTML, every visitor gets the same nonce, and a "number used once" becomes a constant. HTML that carries a nonce shouldn't be shared cached.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Report only, forever.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only&lt;/code&gt; is the right way to start, so you see violations without breaking pages. It is the wrong place to stay. Put a date on flipping to enforce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My take: treat the CSP check like a type check. It's boring, it runs on every PR, and the day it fails is the day it pays for itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Situation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Reach for&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;HTML rendered per request (SSR, PPR dynamic holes)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nonce plus &lt;code&gt;strict-dynamic&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MDN recommends nonces for dynamically generated content&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fully static page whose inline scripts never change&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hashes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No per request value exists to put in the HTML&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Third party script loaded by your own trusted script&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;strict-dynamic&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trust flows from the nonce'd loader to what it loads&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Brand new policy on a busy site&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Report only first, then enforce&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You see violations before users do&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Actionable next steps
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5kw6yc0vs2oek7leed6o.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F5kw6yc0vs2oek7leed6o.png" alt="Upgrade checklist with five items: pin 16.4.4 everywhere, add a PPR check route, verify nonces in the HTML, assert zero violations in a browser, enforce the policy" width="800" height="450"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bump to &lt;code&gt;next@16.4.4&lt;/code&gt; in every app that combines PPR with a nonce based CSP.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add a small &lt;code&gt;/ppr-check&lt;/code&gt; route with one Suspense wrapped dynamic component.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;verify-nonce.mjs&lt;/code&gt; against a production build in CI.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add the Playwright spec so blocked scripts fail the PR, not your users.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If the policy is new, ship it report only, read the reports, then enforce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How does Next.js 16.4.4 CSP nonce PPR work?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Your proxy generates a nonce per request and sends it in the &lt;code&gt;Content-Security-Policy&lt;/code&gt; header. Next.js reads it from the request headers and stamps it on the scripts it renders. With PPR, the static shell is prerendered and an inline resume bootstrap fills the dynamic holes at request time. 16.4.4 backports a fix so that bootstrap gets the nonce too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;When should developers adopt Next.js 16.4.4 CSP nonce PPR?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Now, if you run PPR with a strict nonce CSP. It's a patch release, so the risk is low, but verify it with the two checks above instead of assuming it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Does 16.4.4 include the canary features?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
No. The release notes say it carries specific backports and does not include everything on canary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is CSP Level 3 final?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Not yet. W3C's latest CSP Level 3 publication is a Working Draft, not a Recommendation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Related reading: &lt;a href="https://mudassirworks.hashnode.dev/nextjs-rendering-patterns-rsc-ppr" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js rendering patterns from CSR and SSR to Partial Prerendering&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://mudassirworks.hashnode.dev/next-js-security-hardening-middleware-server-actions-and-csp-patterns-that-hold-up" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js security hardening with middleware, Server Actions and CSP&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;If you want a deeper look at how PPR splits a page into a static shell and dynamic holes, the rendering patterns piece above goes much further than this post.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;If you want a Next.js app with streaming, a strict CSP and AI features wired up end to end, &lt;a href="https://mudassirkhan.me/services/nextjs-for-ai-products" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;that is exactly the kind of Next.js work for AI products I take on&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Drop a comment if your setup looks different. I'm curious what CSP setups people are running with PPR in production.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have you ever wondered what happens when a React component is created, updated, or removed from the screen? React components go through different stages during their lifetime. These stages are known as the &lt;strong&gt;Component Lifecycle.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In modern React, the &lt;strong&gt;useEffect()&lt;/strong&gt; hook helps us perform tasks such as fetching data, updating the document title, setting timers, and handling event listeners.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the Component Lifecycle in React?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The component lifecycle refers to the different stages a component goes through from the moment it appears on the screen until it is removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A React component generally goes through three important stages:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mounting&lt;/strong&gt; – The component is created and displayed on the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Updating&lt;/strong&gt; – The component re-renders when its state or props change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unmounting&lt;/strong&gt; – The component is removed from the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frprg2v8qwh7kaspx6ed9.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frprg2v8qwh7kaspx6ed9.png" alt=" "&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Mounting Phase&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mounting is the stage when a component is created and displayed for the first time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, when we open a webpage and a React component appears on the screen, the component is in its mounting phase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We can use useEffect() to run a function only once when the component initially mounts.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Component Mounted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Hello&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How does this code work?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;useEffect() is a React hook used to perform side effects.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The function inside useEffect() runs after the component renders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The empty dependency array [] tells React to run the effect when the component initially mounts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Updating Phase&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The updating phase occurs when a component re-renders because its state or props have changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We can use useEffect() to run a function whenever a particular state value changes.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Count Updated:"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Increase&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How does this code work?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;useState(0) creates a state variable called count.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;setCount() updates the count value.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When the count changes, the component re-renders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The useEffect() runs whenever the count value changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Unmounting Phase&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unmounting is the stage when a component is removed from the screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sometimes, we need to clean up tasks such as timers, event listeners, or subscriptions when a component is removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We can use the cleanup function inside useEffect().&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Component Mounted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Component Unmounted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Hello&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How does this code work?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The effect runs after the component renders.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The cleanup function is returned from the useEffect().&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The cleanup function runs when the component is removed from the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Understanding the useEffect Dependency Array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
The dependency array determines when the effect runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example 1: Without a dependency array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Runs after every render"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This effect runs after every render, including the initial render and subsequent re-renders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example 2: With an empty dependency array&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Runs after initial render"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This effect runs after the initial render. In development, React Strict Mode may run an additional setup and cleanup cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example 3: With a dependency&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Runs when count changes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This effect runs after the initial render and whenever the count value changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding the React component lifecycle helps us know when components are created, updated, and removed from the screen. The useEffect() hook makes it easier to handle side effects in functional components.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By understanding the dependency array and cleanup function, you can use useEffect() more effectively in your React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Building Market Chart From Scratch: An Open-Source Financial Charting Engine
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What does it take to build a financial charting library from scratch?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not just rendering candlesticks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A serious market charting engine needs to handle rendering performance, large datasets, real-time updates, technical indicators, interactive drawing tools, multiple chart types, persistence, and much more.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to explore these engineering challenges by building an independent charting engine rather than relying entirely on an existing charting solution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's how &lt;strong&gt;Market Chart&lt;/strong&gt; started.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, I'm working on developing it into a modular financial charting ecosystem built with TypeScript, Canvas 2D, and React integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GitHub:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/SinaMohammadi/market-chart" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/SinaMohammadi/market-chart&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;npm:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@market-chart/core" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.npmjs.com/package/@market-chart/core&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Documentation and demos:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://sinamohammadi.github.io/market-chart/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://sinamohammadi.github.io/market-chart/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Why build another charting library?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are already excellent charting solutions available.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So why start from zero?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My motivation wasn't simply to create another candlestick component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to understand the engineering behind a complete financial charting system and have control over its architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of the challenges I wanted to explore included:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Efficient rendering of large financial datasets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Real-time market data updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Extensible technical indicators.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Interactive drawing tools.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiple chart types.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Independent rendering and framework integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Server-side rendering.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Workspace persistence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One architectural principle became particularly important:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The charting engine should not depend on the UI framework that consumes it.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This decision shaped the entire project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  1. Architecture: Separating the Engine from React
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart uses a modular architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The core rendering engine is implemented independently of React, using TypeScript and Canvas 2D.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React integration is provided through a separate package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture looks roughly like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Market Chart
│
├── Core Engine
│   ├── Canvas Renderer
│   ├── Chart Management
│   ├── Scales
│   ├── Events
│   ├── Interaction System
│   └── Plugin System
│
├── React Integration
│   ├── Components
│   ├── Hooks
│   └── Lifecycle Management
│
├── Indicators
├── Drawing Tools
├── Datafeed
├── Persistence
├── Internationalization
├── UI
└── Trading Integrations
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Why separate the engine from React?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because rendering financial data and managing application UI are different responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, receiving a new market price shouldn't require unnecessarily re-rendering an entire React component tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rendering engine should manage its own updates, while React handles application integration and lifecycle management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This separation also makes the core engine more reusable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  2. A Modular TypeScript Ecosystem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I didn't want every feature to live inside one enormous package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, Market Chart is organized into independent packages.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Package&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Responsibility&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/core&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Core rendering engine&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/react&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React integration&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/indicators&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Technical indicators&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/drawing-tools&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Interactive drawing tools&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/charts&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Advanced chart types&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/datafeed&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Market data integrations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/persistence&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Storage and workspace management&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/i18n&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Internationalization&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/ui&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Optional chart interface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/trading&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trading-related visual overlays&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@market-chart/testing&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Testing utilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The advantage is that developers can use the capabilities they actually need.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A project that only requires a basic chart shouldn't necessarily need the complete charting interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  3. Canvas 2D Rendering
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the most interesting parts of this project is the rendering architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead of representing every candle as an individual React element, the core engine uses Canvas 2D.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This provides a different rendering model, particularly useful when dealing with large financial datasets and frequent updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The engine also provides column-based data access using typed arrays.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;series&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;columns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;other&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setColumns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Working with columnar data can reduce object allocation overhead and make certain data-processing operations more efficient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Performance is an ongoing area of development, especially as the project grows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  4. Multiple Financial Chart Types
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Candlestick charts are only one part of financial visualization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes support for several chart representations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Candlestick&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Line&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Area&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Histogram&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Heikin-Ashi&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Renko&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kagi&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Point and Figure&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Line Break&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Footprint&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Heatmap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Volume Profile&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Market Profile&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Different chart types provide different perspectives on market behavior.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal is to make these visualization capabilities available through a consistent charting architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  5. Technical Indicators
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Technical indicators are another major part of the project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes 44 technical indicators.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The indicator architecture is designed around incremental calculations and integration with the chart engine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why is incremental calculation important?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine receiving a new candle every few seconds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Recalculating every historical indicator value after every update can introduce unnecessary work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An incremental calculation model allows the indicator to maintain its previous state and process new data as it arrives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is especially useful for applications displaying continuously updating financial information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  6. Interactive Drawing Tools
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Drawing tools are essential for technical analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Traders use them to identify support and resistance levels, visualize trends, and annotate market structures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes 35 drawing tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The drawing system also supports:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Object selection.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Magnet mode.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Undo and redo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clipboard operations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Serialization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;React integration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A drawing shouldn't just be a collection of pixels on a canvas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should be an independent object with its own state, configuration, and lifecycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes it possible to edit, serialize, restore, and manage drawings separately from the rendering process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  7. Datafeed Architecture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A charting engine shouldn't force developers to use one specific market data provider.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Different exchanges and financial platforms have different APIs and data structures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart provides a datafeed abstraction with support for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Static data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;REST adapters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;WebSocket adapters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Server-Sent Events.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Synthetic data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CSV and JSON imports.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Historical data pagination.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Real-time updates.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This allows developers to connect their own market data infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a cryptocurrency exchange could connect its backend WebSocket service to the chart without coupling the rendering engine to that exchange.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The separation is intentional:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Market Chart handles visualization. Your application controls the market data source.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  8. Persistence and Workspace Management
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful charting application should allow users to save their configurations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Think about a trader who has configured multiple indicators, created drawing objects, and customized several chart layouts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That configuration should be recoverable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The persistence package provides different storage adapters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memory storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Web Storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;IndexedDB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;REST storage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also includes functionality for autosave, versioned schemas, conflict handling, and cross-tab synchronization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The objective is to keep persistence independent of the core rendering engine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  9. React and Next.js Integration
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern financial applications often use React and Next.js.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, integrating browser-based rendering engines into server-rendered applications introduces some interesting challenges.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The chart engine must not assume that browser APIs are available during server-side execution.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes a dedicated SSR entry point:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;StaticChart&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@market-chart/react/ssr'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StaticChart&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"area"&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;history&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;180&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Price, last 90 days"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The project supports static SVG chart rendering on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also provides a progressive approach to chart rendering, allowing an initial representation to be delivered before the interactive chart engine is activated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is particularly useful when building financial dashboards where initial rendering and interactive functionality both matter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  10. Internationalization and RTL
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Internationalization is another feature I wanted to include from the beginning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes support for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;English.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Persian.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Arabic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Custom language registration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Right-to-left layouts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Locale-aware formatting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Translation fallbacks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;RTL support is especially important for financial applications targeting Persian- and Arabic-speaking users.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Internationalization shouldn't be an afterthought added when the interface is already complete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It needs to be considered throughout the architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  11. Optional Full Chart Interface
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some developers want complete control over their chart UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Others want a ready-made interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes an optional UI package with functionality such as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Chart headers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drawing sidebars.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Context menus.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Settings dialogs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keyboard shortcuts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Symbol search.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Saved preferences.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Price actions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The interface also supports extensibility through an action registry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers can register custom actions and adapt the interface to their applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The important part is that the UI remains optional.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can use the underlying engine without adopting the complete interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  12. Trading Integrations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Financial charting and trade execution are separate responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A chart can display open orders, positions, alerts, stop-loss levels, and take-profit levels.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But the actual execution logic belongs to the integrating application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart includes a trading package for visual trading-related overlays and interaction callbacks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It doesn't act as a broker or execute trades.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This keeps the charting infrastructure separate from sensitive financial operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  13. What's Next?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Market Chart is still evolving and currently in its pre-1.0 development stage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is plenty of work ahead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some areas I'm continuing to focus on include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Performance improvements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;API stability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing and visual regression coverage.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Documentation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;More integration examples.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Architecture improvements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developer experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also want to hear from developers who are building financial applications and have real-world charting requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Feedback is extremely valuable at this stage.&lt;/p&gt;




